# Barrage Clones Factory

> One Barrage repo per source repo. This file is the index, not a pack of every project in one page.

## Purpose
Keep a factory that wraps each source repository in its own Barrage clone. The source code stays where it is. The clone only explains it.

## How it works (plain English)
1. List source repos for fitzyracing1.
2. Skip any name that already ends in -barrage.
3. Create `{name}-barrage` as a public repo.
4. Push listing.barrage, SOURCE.md, and a short README.
5. Record the pair in this index.

## Key ideas
- Source repo (the real code)
- Barrage clone (the everyday-language wrap)
- Wave (a batch of clones, because hundreds cannot ship in one pass)

## The actual code
```
for repo in sources:
    if repo.name.endswith("-barrage"): continue
    create repo.name + "-barrage"
    push listing.barrage
    record pair
```

## Line-by-line translation
1. for repo in sources → “walk the account repos”
2. skip -barrage → “do not wrap a wrap”
3. create name-barrage → “make the sibling repo”
4. push listing.barrage → “put the human listing on main”
5. record pair → “write source and clone into the index”

## Decisions the program makes
- If the name already ends in -barrage → skip
- If the clone already exists → update files, do not create a third copy
- If the batch is large → ship a wave and leave the rest queued

## What a person would notice
Separate GitHub pages, one wrap per project, instead of one giant pack.

## Current status
Wave 1 pushed. Hundreds of public sources remain queued.

## Next possible steps
Say "wave 2" to push the next batch of clone repos.
